1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,402
1945.

2
00:00:01,402 --> 00:00:04,132
Manila, in the Philippines. A U.S.

3
00:00:04,132 --> 00:00:08,227
Army film crew records a patient being moved out of

4
00:00:08,227 --> 00:00:09,833
a military hospital.

5
00:00:10,283 --> 00:00:12,164
The patient isn't on a stretcher.

6
00:00:12,164 --> 00:00:13,987
The patient is inside a machine.

7
00:00:13,987 --> 00:00:15,925
A steel tank, loaded onto a plane.

8
00:00:16,375 --> 00:00:18,133
It was called an iron lung.

9
00:00:18,133 --> 00:00:19,370
And it did one job.

10
00:00:19,370 --> 00:00:21,779
It breathed for people who could not.

11
00:00:22,479 --> 00:00:26,122
For decades, the disease that filled these machines

12
00:00:26,122 --> 00:00:27,765
came back every summer.

13
00:00:27,765 --> 00:00:30,694
It was called infantile paralysis. Polio.

14
00:00:31,144 --> 00:00:35,052
Then, in about 25 years, America stopped wild polio

15
00:00:35,052 --> 00:00:37,197
from spreading. This is how.

16
00:00:37,797 --> 00:00:40,751
The iron lung, and the end of polio in America.

17
00:00:43,351 --> 00:00:44,548
Start with the virus.

18
00:00:44,998 --> 00:00:46,570
Polio is caused by a virus.

19
00:00:46,570 --> 00:00:49,250
According to the World Health Organization, it

20
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:52,279
spreads mainly from person to person, through what's

21
00:00:52,279 --> 00:00:53,910
called the fecal-oral route.

22
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:57,327
Most infections cause no symptoms at all.

23
00:00:57,327 --> 00:01:01,090
The CDC says about 70 percent of polio infections in

24
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:02,537
children are silent.

25
00:01:02,537 --> 00:01:06,082
But the World Health Organization says about 1 in

26
00:01:06,082 --> 00:01:09,483
200 infections leads to irreversible paralysis,

27
00:01:09,483 --> 00:01:10,930
usually in the legs.

28
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:14,449
And when the paralysis reached the muscles used for

29
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:17,518
breathing, 5 to 10 percent of those paralyzed died.

30
00:01:17,968 --> 00:01:20,695
In the first half of the twentieth century,

31
00:01:20,695 --> 00:01:23,485
epidemics hit the developed countries of the

32
00:01:23,485 --> 00:01:26,719
Northern Hemisphere every summer and fall, and they

33
00:01:26,719 --> 00:01:27,923
kept getting worse.

34
00:01:27,923 --> 00:01:30,904
In the United States, paralytic polio peaked in

35
00:01:30,904 --> 00:01:33,630
1952, with more than 21,000 cases reported.

36
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:38,343
In the late 1940s and early fifties, according to

37
00:01:38,343 --> 00:01:42,432
the CDC, polio crippled an average of more than

38
00:01:42,432 --> 00:01:45,216
35,000 people a year in the U.S.

39
00:01:45,666 --> 00:01:49,464
Mass Humanities notes severe American epidemics in

40
00:01:49,464 --> 00:01:52,046
nineteen sixteen and in the 1930s.

41
00:01:52,046 --> 00:01:54,628
The most devastating came in 1952.

42
00:01:55,428 --> 00:01:57,949
For the patients who could no longer breathe, there

43
00:01:57,949 --> 00:01:58,641
was a machine.

44
00:01:59,091 --> 00:02:02,091
It was developed at Harvard by Philip Drinker and

45
00:02:02,091 --> 00:02:03,254
Louis Agassiz Shaw.

46
00:02:03,254 --> 00:02:06,132
According to Mass Humanities, the first one was

47
00:02:06,132 --> 00:02:09,193
little more than a galvanized iron box, a bed, and

48
00:02:09,193 --> 00:02:11,029
two household vacuum cleaners.

49
00:02:11,479 --> 00:02:14,953
On October 12, 1928, it was used on a young girl, at

50
00:02:14,953 --> 00:02:16,958
Children's Hospital in Boston.

51
00:02:16,958 --> 00:02:20,098
It was the first successful use of a mechanical

52
00:02:20,098 --> 00:02:21,234
respirator there.

53
00:02:21,684 --> 00:02:22,927
Here's how it worked.

54
00:02:22,927 --> 00:02:25,946
The patient lay inside the tank, with only the head

55
00:02:25,946 --> 00:02:28,433
outside, sealed off by an airtight collar.

56
00:02:28,883 --> 00:02:31,666
When the pressure inside the tank dropped, the chest

57
00:02:31,666 --> 00:02:33,913
expanded, and air streamed into the lungs.

58
00:02:33,913 --> 00:02:36,536
When the pressure rose again, the air flowed out.

59
00:02:36,536 --> 00:02:39,318
Over and over, it did what the patient's own muscles

60
00:02:39,318 --> 00:02:39,854
could not.

61
00:02:40,304 --> 00:02:44,394
In 1931, an improved version was completed by J.H.

62
00:02:44,394 --> 00:02:47,421
Emerson, of Cambridge, Massachusetts.

63
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:51,511
A 1933 account put its price at about $1,000, half

64
00:02:51,511 --> 00:02:54,619
or less the cost of the other machine.

65
00:02:55,069 --> 00:02:58,326
Harvard Medical School says the Emerson model became

66
00:02:58,326 --> 00:03:01,833
the main iron lung used in hospitals across the country.

67
00:03:01,833 --> 00:03:04,589
Boston Children's had a room-sized one, in a

68
00:03:04,589 --> 00:03:05,716
hospital basement.

69
00:03:05,716 --> 00:03:08,659
It could hold five patients at once, and nurses

70
00:03:08,659 --> 00:03:10,851
could walk inside to care for them.

71
00:03:11,301 --> 00:03:14,930
Iron lungs were meant to give temporary relief,

72
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:16,937
until a patient recovered.

73
00:03:16,937 --> 00:03:18,558
But not everyone did.

74
00:03:18,558 --> 00:03:22,264
In 1959, about 1,200 people in the United States

75
00:03:22,264 --> 00:03:24,889
were still using tank respirators.

76
00:03:25,339 --> 00:03:28,030
Caring for someone inside a tank was hard.

77
00:03:28,030 --> 00:03:31,299
Later machines, like a 1957 Emerson model described

78
00:03:31,299 --> 00:03:34,632
by the Kansas Historical Society, had a plastic dome

79
00:03:34,632 --> 00:03:37,901
that could go over the patient's head and take over

80
00:03:37,901 --> 00:03:40,721
the breathing, during bathing or other care.

81
00:03:41,521 --> 00:03:44,530
Polio's most famous patient was a future president.

82
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,529
In August 1921, at 39, Franklin D.

83
00:03:47,529 --> 00:03:51,202
Roosevelt was diagnosed with infantile paralysis.

84
00:03:51,202 --> 00:03:54,875
In 1924, he went to Warm Springs, Georgia, hoping

85
00:03:54,875 --> 00:03:56,524
its waters would help.

86
00:03:56,524 --> 00:03:59,372
Two years later, he bought the resort.

87
00:03:59,822 --> 00:04:03,644
Fundraising for Warm Springs grew into something bigger.

88
00:04:03,644 --> 00:04:07,057
In 1938, Roosevelt founded the National Foundation

89
00:04:07,057 --> 00:04:08,695
for Infantile Paralysis.

90
00:04:09,145 --> 00:04:12,736
The radio comedian Eddie Cantor urged Americans to

91
00:04:12,736 --> 00:04:16,327
send their dimes directly to the president, at the

92
00:04:16,327 --> 00:04:17,188
White House.

93
00:04:17,188 --> 00:04:18,912
And he suggested a name.

94
00:04:18,912 --> 00:04:20,276
The March of Dimes.

95
00:04:20,726 --> 00:04:22,216
Movie audiences gave.

96
00:04:22,216 --> 00:04:25,408
In this nineteen 44 newsreel, a young girl on

97
00:04:25,408 --> 00:04:28,458
crutches thanks moviegoers for their dimes.

98
00:04:28,908 --> 00:04:32,008
And the National Foundation would pick the man to

99
00:04:32,008 --> 00:04:34,982
run what PBS NewsHour calls the largest vaccine

100
00:04:34,982 --> 00:04:36,438
trial of its kind ever.

101
00:04:37,238 --> 00:04:39,119
The vaccine came from Jonas Salk.

102
00:04:39,569 --> 00:04:43,255
In the early 1950s, Salk developed an inactivated

103
00:04:43,255 --> 00:04:44,308
polio vaccine.

104
00:04:44,308 --> 00:04:45,887
It used killed virus.

105
00:04:45,887 --> 00:04:49,724
In this 1956 film, made by the National Foundation,

106
00:04:49,724 --> 00:04:53,635
the vaccine is produced and checked, batch by batch.

107
00:04:54,085 --> 00:04:57,133
To find out if it worked, the National Foundation

108
00:04:57,133 --> 00:05:00,368
turned to Salk's former teacher, Thomas Francis Jr.,

109
00:05:00,368 --> 00:05:02,234
at the University of Michigan.

110
00:05:02,684 --> 00:05:06,195
The trial formally began on April 26, 1954.

111
00:05:06,195 --> 00:05:09,542
More than 1.8 million children took part.

112
00:05:09,542 --> 00:05:13,625
It relied on about 150,000 volunteers, some 15,000

113
00:05:13,625 --> 00:05:16,972
schools, and 44 state health departments.

114
00:05:17,422 --> 00:05:20,520
In part of the trial, some children got the vaccine,

115
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:23,142
and a group at least as large got a placebo.

116
00:05:23,142 --> 00:05:24,750
That part was double-blind.

117
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:27,551
Neither the children nor the doctors giving the

118
00:05:27,551 --> 00:05:29,159
shots knew which was which.

119
00:05:29,609 --> 00:05:33,229
On April 12, 1955, Francis stepped up in Rackham

120
00:05:33,229 --> 00:05:36,999
Auditorium, in Ann Arbor, to announce the results.

121
00:05:37,450 --> 00:05:38,690
The vaccine works.

122
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:41,032
It is safe, effective, and potent.

123
00:05:41,832 --> 00:05:44,921
According to the University of Michigan, it was up

124
00:05:44,921 --> 00:05:47,578
to 80 to 90 percent effective in preventing

125
00:05:47,578 --> 00:05:48,567
paralytic polio.

126
00:05:48,567 --> 00:05:50,049
Newspapers ran the news.

127
00:05:50,499 --> 00:05:54,277
That same year, 1955, the vaccine was licensed for

128
00:05:54,277 --> 00:05:56,166
use in the United States.

129
00:05:56,966 --> 00:05:58,105
Then came a setback.

130
00:05:58,555 --> 00:06:02,666
In 1955, some batches of polio vaccine given to the

131
00:06:02,666 --> 00:06:05,407
public contained live polio virus.

132
00:06:05,407 --> 00:06:09,115
The CDC says more than 250 cases of polio were

133
00:06:09,115 --> 00:06:13,065
attributed to vaccine made by one company, Cutter

134
00:06:13,065 --> 00:06:14,113
Laboratories.

135
00:06:14,563 --> 00:06:17,963
The vaccine was recalled as soon as the cases were

136
00:06:17,963 --> 00:06:18,575
detected.

137
00:06:18,575 --> 00:06:21,974
The government improved the production process and

138
00:06:21,974 --> 00:06:23,334
increased oversight.

139
00:06:23,334 --> 00:06:26,462
By the fall of 1955, vaccinations had resumed.

140
00:06:26,462 --> 00:06:29,997
The CDC says the incident led to a better system for

141
00:06:29,997 --> 00:06:31,357
regulating vaccines.

142
00:06:31,807 --> 00:06:33,314
Vaccination went on.

143
00:06:33,314 --> 00:06:37,156
In this 1957 newsreel, the Salk vaccine is given to

144
00:06:37,156 --> 00:06:39,491
children, including twin girls.

145
00:06:40,291 --> 00:06:42,231
Next, America had to reach everyone.

146
00:06:42,681 --> 00:06:46,159
In 1961, the Communicable Disease Center, today's

147
00:06:46,159 --> 00:06:49,211
CDC, filmed a polio vaccination campaign in

148
00:06:49,211 --> 00:06:52,831
Columbus, Georgia, with the state and county health

149
00:06:52,831 --> 00:06:53,683
departments.

150
00:06:54,133 --> 00:06:55,921
Free vaccine stations went up.

151
00:06:55,921 --> 00:06:58,423
Signs went up on street corners and buses.

152
00:06:58,423 --> 00:06:59,794
The message was simple.

153
00:06:59,794 --> 00:07:00,687
One shot, good.

154
00:07:00,687 --> 00:07:01,760
Two shots, better.

155
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:02,832
Three shots, best.

156
00:07:03,282 --> 00:07:05,893
Health workers went door to door with forms.

157
00:07:05,893 --> 00:07:08,978
And the campaign kept score, charting how many young

158
00:07:08,978 --> 00:07:11,707
children in each area had three or more shots.

159
00:07:12,157 --> 00:07:14,866
Meanwhile, a second vaccine arrived.

160
00:07:14,866 --> 00:07:18,477
The CDC credits Albert Sabin with developing the

161
00:07:18,477 --> 00:07:21,412
oral polio vaccine, in the early 1960s.

162
00:07:21,412 --> 00:07:24,346
Oral vaccines were licensed in the U.S.

163
00:07:24,346 --> 00:07:25,626
starting in 1961.

164
00:07:26,076 --> 00:07:26,910
It worked.

165
00:07:26,910 --> 00:07:31,001
According to the CDC, the last cases of paralytic

166
00:07:31,001 --> 00:07:35,175
polio caused by locally acquired wild virus in the

167
00:07:35,175 --> 00:07:38,180
United States were reported in 1979.

168
00:07:38,630 --> 00:07:42,182
Since 2000, the only polio vaccine used in the U.S.

169
00:07:42,182 --> 00:07:45,386
has been the inactivated one, given as a shot.

170
00:07:45,386 --> 00:07:48,729
The CDC notes that because it's made from killed

171
00:07:48,729 --> 00:07:50,748
virus, it cannot cause polio.

172
00:07:51,548 --> 00:07:53,478
But what replaced the iron lung?

173
00:07:53,478 --> 00:07:55,226
That story starts in Denmark.

174
00:07:55,676 --> 00:07:58,175
In 1952, polio hit Copenhagen hard.

175
00:07:58,175 --> 00:08:01,244
Two accounts of the epidemic say the city's

176
00:08:01,244 --> 00:08:04,956
infectious disease hospital, and Denmark as a whole,

177
00:08:04,956 --> 00:08:06,598
had just one iron lung.

178
00:08:07,048 --> 00:08:10,211
A Danish anesthesiologist, Bjørn Ibsen, suggested

179
00:08:10,211 --> 00:08:11,309
another approach.

180
00:08:11,309 --> 00:08:14,472
Instead of pulling air in from the outside of the

181
00:08:14,472 --> 00:08:17,829
body, push air into the lungs, through a tube in the

182
00:08:17,829 --> 00:08:18,410
windpipe.

183
00:08:18,410 --> 00:08:19,572
Positive pressure.

184
00:08:20,022 --> 00:08:22,374
But there weren't machines to do the pushing.

185
00:08:22,374 --> 00:08:24,726
So people did it by hand, squeezing a bag, in

186
00:08:24,726 --> 00:08:26,033
shifts, around the clock.

187
00:08:26,033 --> 00:08:28,646
Some accounts put the number of medical and dental

188
00:08:28,646 --> 00:08:31,102
students who took part at more than a thousand.

189
00:08:31,552 --> 00:08:35,213
Deaths among the patients with breathing paralysis

190
00:08:35,213 --> 00:08:36,164
fell sharply.

191
00:08:36,164 --> 00:08:39,898
And in December 1953, the first intensive care unit

192
00:08:39,898 --> 00:08:41,435
opened in Copenhagen.

193
00:08:41,885 --> 00:08:45,226
Harvard Medical School says iron lungs eventually

194
00:08:45,226 --> 00:08:46,316
became obsolete.

195
00:08:46,316 --> 00:08:49,521
Today, intensive care units like these, at U.S.

196
00:08:49,521 --> 00:08:52,793
Naval Hospital Guam, use mechanical ventilators.

197
00:08:53,243 --> 00:08:56,918
And here, an Air Force respiratory care practitioner

198
00:08:56,918 --> 00:09:00,239
teaches Tanzanian military medical staff to use

199
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:03,631
ventilators, during a training exercise in 2026.

200
00:09:04,431 --> 00:09:06,927
The rest of the world still had to finish the job.

201
00:09:07,377 --> 00:09:11,151
In 1994, an international commission certified the

202
00:09:11,151 --> 00:09:14,925
Western Hemisphere free of native wild poliovirus.

203
00:09:15,375 --> 00:09:18,826
Globally, the push had begun in 1988, with the

204
00:09:18,826 --> 00:09:21,526
Global Polio Eradication Initiative.

205
00:09:21,526 --> 00:09:25,352
Back then, the World Health Organization estimates,

206
00:09:25,352 --> 00:09:28,952
there were about 350,000 cases, in more than 125

207
00:09:28,952 --> 00:09:29,703
countries.

208
00:09:29,703 --> 00:09:33,303
Since then, wild polio cases have fallen by more

209
00:09:33,303 --> 00:09:34,503
than 99 percent.

210
00:09:34,953 --> 00:09:38,739
Of the three types of wild poliovirus, two have been

211
00:09:38,739 --> 00:09:40,196
declared eradicated.

212
00:09:40,196 --> 00:09:43,181
Type two in 2015, and type three in 2019.

213
00:09:43,631 --> 00:09:47,323
Today, according to the World Health Organization,

214
00:09:47,323 --> 00:09:50,572
wild polio is endemic in only two countries.

215
00:09:50,572 --> 00:09:52,492
Afghanistan, and Pakistan.

216
00:09:53,292 --> 00:09:55,926
So, how did wild polio end in America?

217
00:09:56,376 --> 00:09:57,957
Not with the iron lung.

218
00:09:57,957 --> 00:10:00,020
The machine kept people alive.

219
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:01,945
It could not stop the virus.

220
00:10:02,395 --> 00:10:04,400
That took a nation sending dimes.

221
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:07,620
A trial with almost 2 million children. Two vaccines.

222
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:09,868
And campaigns that went door to door.

223
00:10:10,318 --> 00:10:13,357
And the crisis the iron lung couldn't handle helped

224
00:10:13,357 --> 00:10:15,681
give us the modern intensive care unit.

225
00:10:16,131 --> 00:10:17,513
Next time on KEPT ALIVE.

226
00:10:17,513 --> 00:10:19,816
Whole blood, and the walking blood bank.

227
00:10:20,616 --> 00:10:21,756
For education only.

228
00:10:21,756 --> 00:10:23,377
This is not medical advice.

229
00:10:23,377 --> 00:10:26,198
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
